    "q-64674": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64674",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The angle $\\theta ={1032}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={672}^{∘}$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={312}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${312}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/6db0d45744872518e73e249bc793e48792202c100bf42739fc889febb0fd6c32.png)\n\nWe see that ${312}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, ${1032}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
