    "q-64828": {
      "dom_id": "step-q64828",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The angle $\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/d00b1363ef23bfd1e84ae968e1dc6ec4e36aa7f45d469c653b9f930cb0705cdf.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-64828-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III",
