    "q-124720": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124720",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/4a6bb0dd4991fe1a00bfedc7814f27c4a36b4114fa027673ed9cd03856fcc0d5.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-124720-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III",
              "html": "III"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-124720-b",
              "type": "text",
